codecache: a cache that cannot be written must not be half-written.

SaveData writes the header, the block list, the relocation table and the file
padding before anything checks them. The only check was ApplyCodeRelocations,
five writes later, whose failure path is

    LOGMAN_THROW_A_FMT(false, "Failed to apply code relocations");
    return false;

and LOGMAN_THROW_A_FMT is compiled out of the Release build this ships. So a
rejected cache was a silent return that left a file holding a header, a block
list and a relocation table with no code section behind them. The next launch
mmaps it, walks into the code that is not there, and refuses it.

MEASURED on Resident Evil Requiem, in every session in the corpus:

    Code cache relocation 1863801 offset 0x6630e70 (base 0x0) does not fit a
      107151044 byte buffer; refusing to apply           <- generation
    Code cache relocation 1878114 of 7952524 targets 0x66f403c, outside a
      0x66f4000 byte code buffer                         <- load
    Failed to load cache: re9.exe-a7b10b28eeac3098

Roughly 74 seconds of ahead-of-time compilation and ~100 MB written, discarded
on load, every launch -- with the game running on the runtime JIT throughout
while appearing to have a warm cache.

WHY THE TABLE IS BAD was already written down under 0011, as an inference drawn
after the corpus had been cleared. These logs are the observation that confirms
it: when generation exhausts the code buffer, JIT.cpp calls ClearCodeCache,
which installs a fresh buffer and restarts LatestOffset at zero, while
relocations are deliberately kept across that point. Everything recorded before
the swap keeps an offset into a buffer that no longer exists.

Raising MAX_CODE_SIZE is not the fix, and its own comment says why: "we don't
want to move above 128MB atm because that means we will have to encode longer
jumps". That is the +/-128 MB reach of an AArch64 b/bl, not a preference.

A cache that spans a swap cannot be repaired at this point -- the code those
offsets referred to is gone. What is fixed here is writing it anyway. The same
invariants the loader enforces are checked before the first write, so the
failure costs an empty file and one line that says which invariant failed,
instead of a hundred megabytes that look valid until the next launch.

MaxRelocationSize moves above SaveData because SaveData is defined before the
point 0011 introduced it.

diff --git a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/CodeCache.cpp b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/CodeCache.cpp
--- a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/CodeCache.cpp
+++ b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/CodeCache.cpp
@@ -303,6 +303,11 @@
